<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vaison-la-Romaine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vasio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vaison-la-Romaine is a town on the Ouvèze in the Vaucluse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Provence, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As Vasio it was one of the two capitals of the Vocontii and a wealthy Roman town, and its large excavated remains include houses with mosaics, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and a Roman bridge still in use. A medieval upper town and castle stand across the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vaison-la-romaine.jpg" alt="Vaison-la-Romaine" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FrogsLegs71 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pont_Romain_Roman_Bridge_in_Vaison-la-Romaine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vaison-la-Romaine lies in the Vaucluse department, in the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region. The Roman ruins stand in the valley on the banks of the Ouvèze, which is crossed by a Roman bridge of the 1st century CE, while the medieval town stands high on a rocky hill. The area was inhabited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. At the end of the 4th century BCE Vaison became the capital of a Celtic tribe, the Vocontii, centred on the oppidum in the upper city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Roman conquest of 125–118 BCE in the wars against the Salyes, the Vocontii kept a degree of autonomy. They had two capitals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luc-en-diois">Luc-en-Diois</a>, apparently the religious centre, and Vaison, named Vasio Julia Vocontiorum. Early building was probably done by Vocontian aristocrats who moved down from the oppidum and set their houses along the river. Large public monuments, including a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, a bridge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and thermal baths, were begun in the second half of the 1st century. The city was at its finest in the 2nd century, when it covered up to 75 hectares and was one of the richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>.</p><p>Pillaging and burning in 276 were followed by recovery. A bishopric existed from the 4th century, and councils met there in 442 and 529. Invasions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> in the 5th century ruined the city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>'s benches began to be reused as Christian tombstones. Vaison was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in 527 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a>, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, in 545. In the 12th century the counts of Provence, who had refortified the upper town, quarrelled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, each of whom held half the town, until a treaty of 1251 negotiated by the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-iv">Pope Clement IV</a>. From the 18th century most of the population moved back down to the plain by the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Roman ruins lie in two main areas, La Villasse and Puymin. Several large town houses have been excavated, among them the House of the Dolphin, the House of the Laurelled Apollo, the House of the Arbour and the House of the Peacock, which must have belonged to Vocontian aristocrats who owned estates in the region. The apse of the Church of St. Quenin seems to date from the 8th century. A market authorized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> in 1483 is still held on Tuesdays.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vaison-la-Romaine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After submission they kept unusual autonomy as a federated people, with two capitals, <b>Vasio</b> (Vaison-la-Romaine) and Lucus Augusti (Luc-en-Diois), later replaced by Dea Augusta (Die).</blockquote><a href="/state/vocontii">Vocontii</a></li></ul></section>
